!set bg #08111F
!set color #F4F1E8
!set accent #5DE4C7
!set font Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif
!set headfont Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif
!set linkcolor #CCCCFF
!set visitedlinkcolor #CCCCFF
!set size 30

!set palette focus #FFD166
!set palette id #8CD9FF
!set palette done #B6A0D6
!set palette emp #80FF80
!set palette explain #DF97FF

!style note {size=27px, w=1112px}
!style code {font=Menlo, size=23px, w=740px, pad=12px, fill=#102233, color=#F4F1E8, stroke=#31505F}
!style node {size=22px, pad=0px, align=center, fill=#173749, stroke=#8CD9FF, textcolor=#F4F1E8, thick=2}
!style edge {color=#8AA5B5, thick=2}

# CSE450 Translation of Programming Languages
## Lecture 10: Writing Code in WebAssembly Text (WAT)
---
### What is WebAssembly?
[wasm] @(84, 185) {$note, w=1100px, size=32px} **{{color=emp|WebAssembly (Wasm)}}** is a portable binary format designed for safe, efficient web development.
...
[browsers] @(84, 300) {$note, w=1100px, size=30px} Wasm has implementations in all major web browsers.
...
[wat] @(84, 390) {$note, w=1100px, size=32px} **{{color=emp|WebAssembly Text (WAT)}}** is a human-readable text format that can be converted to Wasm.
...
[compilers] @(84, 535) {$note, w=1100px, size=30px} Compilers can target Wasm, including Emscripten for C/C++ and toolchains for Rust.
---
### Example WebAssembly Text Program
[module] @(70, 170) {$code, w=740px, size=23px} ```
(module







)
```
...
[f0] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,182.0) (730,32.199999999999996)
[explain] @(850, 185) {$note, w=345px, size=27px, color=explain} Begin with a `module` form. Parentheses delimit the module and its nested forms.
...
!anim f0 hide 0.15s
[f1] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,439.59999999999997) (730,32.199999999999996)
!anim explain text The closing parenthesis ends the module.
...
!anim f1 hide 0.15s
!anim module hide 0.15s
[skeleton] @(70, 170) {$code, w=740px, size=23px} ```
(module
  (func $Fun1
    (param $var0 f64) (param $var1 f64)
    (result f64)

    ;; Function body goes here
  )

)
```
[f2] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,214.2) (730,32.199999999999996)
!anim explain text `func` introduces a function.
...
!anim explain text `$Fun1` is its internal WAT identifier.
...
!anim f2 hide 0.15s
[f3] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,246.39999999999998) (730,32.199999999999996)
!anim explain text Each `param` gives a parameter name and type.
...
!anim explain text `f64` is a 64-bit floating-point type.
...
!anim f3 hide 0.15s
[f4] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,278.6) (730,32.199999999999996)
!anim explain text `result` gives the function’s return type.
...
!anim f4 hide 0.15s
!anim skeleton hide 0.15s
[exported] @(70, 170) {$code, w=740px, size=23px} ```
(module
  (func $Fun1
    (param $var0 f64) (param $var1 f64)
    (result f64)

    ;; Function body goes here
  )
  (export "CalcHypotenuse" (func $Fun1))
)
```
[f5] {color=focus, thick=3, fill=none, round=0} !rect (75,407.4) (730,32.199999999999996)
!anim explain text `export` exposes the function as `CalcHypotenuse` to JavaScript.
...
[purpose] @(850, 440) {$note, w=345px, size=27px, color=emp} Two sides of a right triangle go in. The hypotenuse length comes out.
