### The Operand Stack
[context] @(70, 150) {$note, w=1120px, size=25px} Inside `$Fun1`, with parameters `$var0` and `$var1` of type `f64`.
[label] @(850, 350) {$note, w=345px, size=25px, color=id} Stack (bottom → top)
[stack] @(850, 395) {$note, w=345px, size=31px, color=emp} [ ]
[explain] @(850, 195) {$note, w=345px, size=27px, color=explain} Instructions consume and produce stack values.
...
[op0] @(70, 190) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
local.get $var0
```
!anim explain text Push the first parameter.
!anim stack text [ a ]
...
[op1] @(70, 250) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
local.get $var0
```
!anim explain text Push the first parameter again.
!anim stack text [ a, a ]
...
[op2] @(70, 310) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
f64.mul
```
!anim explain text Pop two values, multiply, then push the product.
!anim stack text [ a² ]
...
[op3] @(70, 370) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
local.get $var1
```
!anim explain text Push the second parameter.
!anim stack text [ a², b ]
...
[op4] @(70, 430) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
local.get $var1
```
!anim explain text Push the second parameter again.
!anim stack text [ a², b, b ]
...
[op5] @(70, 490) {$code, w=740px, size=26px, pad=6px} ```
f64.mul
```
!anim explain text Pop two values, multiply, then push the product.
!anim stack text [ a², b² ]
